 \pi_1(R)=R^{-5/48+o(1)},\qquad
 \pi_{2k}(r,R)=R^{-(4k^2-1)/12+o(1)}
 \qquad(R\to\infty).$$ Moreover, $$\label{intro:bond-thermodynamic-exponents}
\begin{aligned}
 \theta(p)&=(p-\tfrac12)^{5/36+o(1)}
   &&(p\downarrow\tfrac12),\\
 \chi(p)&=|p-\tfrac12|^{-43/18+o(1)}
   &&(p\to\tfrac12,\ p\ne\tfrac12),\\
 \xi(p)&=|p-\tfrac12|^{-4/3+o(1)}
   &&(p\to\tfrac12,\ p\ne\tfrac12).
\end{aligned}$$

In any fixed buffered annulus or sector of the preceding types, a raw passage count $X$ satisfies $$\label{prelim:passage-moment}
 \mathbb E[\exp(BX)\mid\text{exterior}]\le C_B
 \qquad(0\le B<\infty),$$ uniformly in scale and in exterior states inducing the specified graph and arbitrary boundary partition. The same holds for a fixed finite family of macroscopic passage counts in a fixed geometry.

Suppose $Y_n\ge0$, $\mathbb E Y_n\ge n^{-A}/C$, and $\mathbb E Y_n^2\le Cn^A$, whereas $X_n\ge0$ has uniformly bounded exponential moments at every fixed rate. For fixed $C_0\ge1$ and every $\varepsilon>0$, $$C_\varepsilon^{-1}n^{-\varepsilon}
 \le
 \frac{\mathbb E[Y_n C_0^{\pm X_n}]}{\mathbb E Y_n}
 \le C_\varepsilon n^{\varepsilon}.$$

With the definitions above, $$\begin{aligned}
 2P+2D_*+4(C_0+C_1)&\le o(\log n),\label{exp:rectangle-budget}\\
 C_0+C_1+L_0+L_1&\le o(\log n),\label{exp:corner-budget}\\
